.TH CPUZ-LINUX 1 "October 2026" "0.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cpuz-linux \- experimental single and multi thread CPU benchmark
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cpuz-linux
[\fB--tui\fR] [\fB--mode single|multi|both\fR]
[\fB--seconds N\fR] [\fB--threads auto|N\fR] [\fB--json\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Runs a scalar float32 procedural-noise workload based on static investigation
of CPU-Z 17.01 x64. This is experimental software, not an official CPU-Z port.
Without arguments, opens the TUI if stdin and stdout are terminals; otherwise
runs a 10-second single test. Scores count completed pixels per millisecond.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --tui
Open interactive terminal interface. Requires a TTY.
.TP
.B --no-tui
Use text output.
.TP
.B --mode single|multi|both
Select the test. Both runs multi followed by single, each for the requested time.
.TP
.B --seconds N
Duration per phase, from 0.05 to 3600. Default 10.
.TP
.B --threads auto|N
Multi workers, from 1 to 256. Default: available CPUs, capped at 256.
Single always uses one compute worker.
.TP
.B --json
Emit a structured JSON report and disable the TUI.
.TP
.B --help
Show help and exit.
.SH KEYS
S single, M multi, B both, + and - adjust workers, [ and ] adjust seconds,
C cancels, Q or Escape exits. Ctrl+C cancels and exits.
.SH MEASUREMENT
All multi workers share a start and deadline. Sum counted pixels and divide
by common elapsed integer milliseconds. Initialization and checksums are
outside timing. No reference CPU normalization is applied.
.SH LIMITATIONS
Numerical and performance equivalence with CPU-Z has not been established.
Linux scheduling, libm, compiler output, affinity, power and thermal state
can change results. Multi is a Linux adaptation of the inspected workload.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 success; 2 invalid arguments, terminal errors or benchmark failures.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR taskset (1),
.BR lscpu (1)
.SH PROJECT
https://github.com/kimlherme/cpuz-linux
